            0.064
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A Tale of Three Intersecting Lines"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.35,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exterior Angles"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.12,
            0.848,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The angle formed between the extension of a side of a triangle and the other side is called an "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "exterior angle",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the triangle. In this figure, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  ACD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an exterior angle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.324,
            0.187,
            0.674,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.324,
                0.187,
                0.674,
                0.328
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_21_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.351,
            0.521,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Find "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  ACD ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , if "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{A} = 50^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{B} = 60^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.382,
            0.614,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the angle sum property, we know that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.379,
            0.41,
            0.617,
            0.43
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_21_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "50^{\\circ}+60^{\\circ}+\\angle ACB=180^{\\circ}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.447,
            0.597,
            0.466
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_21_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "110^{\\circ}+\\angle ACB=180^{\\circ}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.471,
            0.328,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACB = 70^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.49,
            0.463,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACD = 180^{\\circ} - 70^{\\circ} = 110^{\\circ},"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.509,
            0.846,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "since, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { A C B }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  ACD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " together form a straight angle. Find the exterior angle for different measures of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { A }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { B }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Do you see any relation between the exterior angle and these two angles? "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "[Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " From angle sum property, we have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{B} + \\angle \\mathrm{ACB} = 180^{\\circ}."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " We also have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACD + \\angle ACB = 180^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , since they form a straight angle. What does this show?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.643,
            0.847,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.4 Constructions Related to Altitudes of Triangles"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.677,
            0.847,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There is another set of useful measurements with respect to a triangle the height of each of its vertices with respect to the opposite sides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.715,
            0.574,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the world around us, we talk of the heights of various objects: the height of a person, the height of a tree, the height of a building, etc. What do we mean by the word ‘height’?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.806,
            0.572,
            0.861
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider a triangle ABC. What is the height of the vertex A from its opposite side BC, and how can it be measured?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.592,
            0.72,
            0.846,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 16,
              "bbox": [
                0.592,
                0.72,
                0.846,
                0.844
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_21_image_16.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.688,
                0.845,
                0.754,
                0.859
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.8"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.861,
            0.848,
            0.918
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let AD be the line segment from A drawn perpendicular to BC. The length of AD is the height of the vertex A from BC. The line segment AD is said to be one of the ‘altitudes’ of the triangle. The other altitudes"
            }
          ]
        },
